# Chronic Venous Insufficiency

- **Definition** - spectrum of venous disorders attributed to ambulatory venous hypertension:
  - <u>Congenital venous insufficiency</u> - anatomical variants at birth, e.g. venous ectasia, absence of venous valves, Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome
  - <u>Secondary venous insufficiency</u> - a sequelae of previous venous thromboembolism (post-thrombotic syndrome \[PTS\])
  - <u>Primary venous insufficiency</u> - etiology is not clearly identified
- **Risk factors of CVI:**
  - <u>Demographic</u> - advancing age, female gender (hormone-related changes)
  - <u>+ve FHx</u> - some hereditary component (autosomal dominant penetrance) to primary venous insufficiency
  - <u>Progesterone</u> (multiparity) - progesterone causes venous dilatation which causes valvular dysfunction
  - <u>Hx of trauma</u> - Hx of trauma to the extremities
  - <u>Prolonged standing</u> - esp. positive FHx
- **Diagnostic evaluation of venous dysfunction:**
  - <u>Dupplex ultrasound</u>

